_runs/cell_1/experiment2/model_loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:9:{"name": "weak_sampling_concept_elimination", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People generalize by weak-sampling hypothesis elimination: they entertain the same kinds of candidate concepts (mathematical rules and numeric intervals) but treat the examples only as positive labels on numbers that happened to come up, not as draws from the concept, so every concept consistent with the examples stays equally plausible regardless of its size (no size principle). \"Yes\" is the prior-weighted share of the still-consistent concepts that contain the target, with an occasional example tolerated as an error; small, tight concepts get no advantage, so generalization is far broader than under strong sampling.", "context": "experiment1 round 1 candidate 2 lens 5"}
_runs/cell_1/experiment2/model_loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:13:{"name": "tempered_evidence_concept_guessing", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of concept_learning_with_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100, with the same occasional guessing, but they do not weigh the examples as fully independent random draws from the concept — each example's evidence counts for an inferred fraction (or multiple) of a full draw. The one change is this tempered size principle: weighting the evidence below one keeps broad rules and wide intervals plausible and widens generalization, while weighting it above one makes people lock onto the tightest concept consistent with the examples.", "context": "experiment1 round 1 candidate 4 refine incumbent concept_learning_with_guessing repair 1"}
_runs/cell_1/experiment2/model_loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:18:{"name": "redundant_examples_tempered_concepts", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of tempered_evidence_concept_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100, with tempered evidence and occasional guessing, but they treat additional examples as partly redundant rather than as independent draws, so the total evidence from a set grows as an inferred power of the number of examples instead of in proportion to it. The one change is this diminishing (or accelerating) return to set size: with sublinear growth, a single example already supports a fairly tight concept while three or four examples sharpen it much less than strong sampling predicts; an exponent of one recovers the incumbent.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 3 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_concept_guessing"}
_runs/cell_1/experiment2/model_loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:20:{"name": "tempered_evidence_decision_gain", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of decision_gain_concept_learning: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples) and turn their belief into a yes/no answer through the same inferred decision gain on the log-odds, but they do not weigh the examples as fully independent draws from the concept — the one change, taken from the incumbent, is that each example's evidence counts for an inferred fraction (or multiple) of a full draw. Tempering thus decides which concepts people entertain (how broadly they generalize), while the decision gain alone decides how they hedge between belief and answer, with no flat guessing floor: targets with near-zero belief still get near-zero yeses, unlike under the incumbent's guessing.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 5 refine chosen"}
_runs/cell_1/experiment2/model_loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:28:{"name": "redundant_examples_decision_gain", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of tempered_evidence_decision_gain: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples, tempered evidence) and turn their belief into a yes/no answer through the same inferred decision gain on its log-odds, but they treat additional examples as partly redundant rather than as independent draws. The one change, taken from redundant_examples_tempered_concepts, is that the total evidence from a set grows as an inferred power of the number of examples instead of in proportion to it: with sublinear growth, a single example already supports a fairly tight concept while three or four examples sharpen generalization much less than strong sampling predicts (an exponent of one recovers the incumbent).", "context": "experiment1 round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_decision_gain repair 1"}
_runs/cell_1/experiment2/model_loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:32:{"name": "criterion_shifted_redundant_examples", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of criterion_shifted_tempered_decision: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples, tempered evidence) and turn their belief into a yes/no answer through the same decision gain and shifted (biased) criterion on its log-odds, but they treat additional examples as partly redundant rather than as independent draws. The one change, taken from redundant_examples_decision_gain, is that the total evidence from a set grows as an inferred power of the number of examples instead of in proportion to it, so with sublinear growth one example already supports a fairly tight concept while three or four sharpen generalization less than strong sampling predicts, while the criterion shift separately sets the overall lean toward \"yes\" (an exponent of one recovers the incumbent).", "context": "experiment1 round 4 candidate 3 refine incumbent criterion_shifted_tempered_decision"}
_runs/cell_1/experiment2/model_loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:45:{"name": "weak_sampling_concept_elimination", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "95.3 nats behind criterion_shifted_redundant_examples (5.5× dse)", "hypothesis": "People generalize by weak-sampling hypothesis elimination: they entertain the same kinds of candidate concepts (mathematical rules and numeric intervals) but treat the examples only as positive labels on numbers that happened to come up, not as draws from the concept, so every concept consistent with the examples stays equally plausible regardless of its size (no size principle). \"Yes\" is the prior-weighted share of the still-consistent concepts that contain the target, with an occasional example tolerated as an error; small, tight concepts get no advantage, so generalization is far broader than under strong sampling.", "context": "experiment1 end of experiment"}

